Region III shows the greatest richness because it represents the tropical latitudes. Tropical environments:
- remained relatively undisturbed for millions of years (unlike temperate regions, which had frequent glaciations), so there was a long evolutionary time for species diversification;
- are less seasonal, relatively more constant and predictable, which promotes niche specialisation;
- receive more solar energy, so productivity is higher, which can support greater diversity.

Region I shows the least richness because it represents a temperate (higher-latitude) region:
- it was subjected to frequent glaciations in the past, leaving less evolutionary time for species diversification;
- it is more seasonal, with a less constant and less predictable environment;
- it receives less solar energy, so productivity is lower and less diversity can be supported.

(i) Development of the male gametophyte:
- Each cell of the sporogenous tissue of the anther (the microspore mother cell) undergoes meiosis to form a cluster of four haploid microspores, the microspore tetrad. This is microsporogenesis.
- As the anther matures and dehydrates, the microspores separate and develop into pollen grains.
- The pollen grain is the male gametophyte. Its single cell divides asymmetrically (mitosis) into a large **vegetative cell**, with abundant food reserves and an irregular nucleus, and a small spindle-shaped **generative cell** that floats in its cytoplasm.
- The generative cell divides mitotically to form **two male gametes**, either before pollen is shed (a 3-celled pollen grain) or later, inside the pollen tube.

(ii) A three-celled male gametophyte (mature pollen grain) is drawn as a rounded grain with these labels:
- **exine**, the tough outer wall (of sporopollenin);
- **intine**, the thin inner wall;
- **germ pore**, the gap in the exine where sporopollenin is absent;
- **vegetative cell** with its nucleus;
- **two male gametes**.
